<!--
  THE ONE MANIFEST IN THIS DIRECTORY THAT IS NOT EMPTY, and every line of it
  earns its place.

  `phone-setup` and `tracker-watchdog` both declare nothing, because everything
  they need is either already merged or lives in `app.json` — the standing rule
  that every permission this app asks a salesman for is readable in ONE file.
  That rule is kept here too: THERE IS NOT A SINGLE `uses-permission` BELOW.
  What cannot live in app.json is a component, and this module is three of them.

  THE FOUR PERMISSIONS THIS MODULE NEEDS ARE ALREADY GRANTED TO THE APP, by
  `expo-location`'s own config plugin, from the options already set in
  `app.json`:

    ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION           declared in expo-location's own manifest
    ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION     added by `isAndroidBackgroundLocationEnabled`
    FOREGROUND_SERVICE             added by `isAndroidForegroundServiceEnabled`
    FOREGROUND_SERVICE_LOCATION    added by the same option

  That was VERIFIED in `node_modules/expo-location/plugin/build/withLocation.js`
  rather than assumed, because a foreground service of type `location` that is
  missing FOREGROUND_SERVICE_LOCATION does not warn: on API 34 it throws
  SecurityException at `startForeground`, inside a service started from a
  background worker, which is the exact shape of failure nobody ever sees.

  `RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED` IS ALSO ALREADY THERE, and this is the one that
  surprises people. `androidx.work:work-runtime` declares it in its own
  manifest; `expo-background-task` has depended on WorkManager since it was
  added, so the permission has been merged into this app since long before this
  module existed. Declaring it again would read, to the next person, as the
  thing that made boot work.

  WHAT IS NOT ALREADY THERE IS A RECEIVER THAT STARTS *THIS* SERVICE.
  androidx.work's own `RescheduleReceiver` re-enqueues periodic work, and
  expo-task-manager's `TaskBroadcastReceiver` reconstructs the expo task —
  neither of them knows this service exists. So the receiver below is the third
  restart layer and not a fourth copy of anything.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
  <application>

    <!--
      THE SERVICE ITSELF.

      `exported="false"` because nothing outside this app may start it: it
      records where a salesman is, and a component another app could poke is a
      component another app can turn into a tracker.

      `foregroundServiceType="location"` is what makes `startForeground` legal
      at all from API 29, and from API 34 it is checked against the permission
      list as well. It is declared HERE and passed AGAIN as the third argument
      to `startForeground` — the manifest declares what the service may be, the
      call declares what this start actually is, and on API 34 both are read.

      `stopWithTask="false"` is the load-bearing one and it is the whole reason
      this exists rather than a task. A salesman swiping MahekOne off the
      recents list is the ordinary way an Android phone is tidied up, and it
      must not be the way a working day stops being recorded. The trail already
      has one honest end — the check-out — and only that may stop it.

      There is no `android:process`, deliberately. A separate process would
      give the service its own memory and make it survive a JS crash, and it
      would also mean two processes reading one SQLite file, which is the
      problem `FixStore` exists to avoid rather than to introduce.
    -->
    <service
      android:name="expo.modules.locationservice.MbosLocationService"
      android:exported="false"
      android:stopWithTask="false"
      android:foregroundServiceType="location" />

    <!--
      RESTART LAYER THREE: the phone was switched off and on again.

      `BOOT_COMPLETED` is also one of the documented exemptions from Android
      12's restriction on starting a foreground service from the background —
      which is the restriction the whole of `ServiceLauncher` is written
      around — so this receiver is not merely allowed to run, it is one of the
      few moments a cold process is allowed to put the service back.

      `MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED` is the same fact after an app update rather than a
      reboot: a sideloaded APK replaces the running one, every process is
      killed, and a day that was open at the moment of the install would
      otherwise record nothing until somebody opened the app. This app is
      installed by hand, over the top, on working phones; that is not a rare
      case here.

      `QUICKBOOT_POWERON` is HTC's and some Chinese ROMs' own spelling of a
      boot broadcast. It costs one line and catches the handsets least likely
      to send the standard one.
    -->
    <receiver
      android:name="expo.modules.locationservice.BootReceiver"
      android:exported="false"
      android:directBootAware="false">
      <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED" />
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED" />
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.QUICKBOOT_POWERON" />
      </intent-filter>
    </receiver>

  </application>
</manifest>
